    category: 'before-you-go',
    summary: 'Almost every visitor can buy a tourist visa on landing at Tribhuvan International Airport. Here are the real fees, the 15, 30 and 90-day options, the documents to carry and the handful of nationalities that must apply in advance.',
    content: 'Nepal is one of the easiest countries in Asia to enter. Nationals of almost every country can buy a tourist visa on arrival at Tribhuvan International Airport in Kathmandu, at Pokhara and Bhairahawa international airports, and at the main land borders with India and Tibet. There is no invitation letter, no sponsor and no interview. You land, you fill in a form, you queue, and you walk out with a visa sticker in your passport. For a standard trek or tour with us, this is all you will ever need.\n\nThree tourist visa lengths are sold, and the fees have been stable for years: 15 days for USD 30, 30 days for USD 50, and 90 days for USD 125. The clock starts on the day you enter, not the day you first trek, so count your acclimatisation days, buffer days and any post-trek time in Kathmandu before you choose. A 14-day itinerary such as our Everest Base Camp Trek fits inside a 15-day visa only if your international flights land and leave on the same days as the trip — most travellers are more comfortable buying the 30-day visa and keeping the spare days for weather delays out of Lukla.\n\nYour passport must be machine-readable and valid for at least six months beyond your arrival date, with at least one clear page for the sticker. Bring the visa fee in clean, undamaged US dollar notes; the counters at Tribhuvan accept cash in several major currencies but the exchange is unkind and torn or heavily marked notes are refused. Carry one passport-size photograph as a backup even though the arrival kiosks photograph you on the spot, because the kiosks queue badly when three wide-body aircraft land together.\n\nSince the Department of Immigration moved its arrival form online, the fastest route through the airport is to complete the tourist visa application on the immigration portal within fifteen days of travel, print or screenshot the confirmation barcode, and take it straight to the fee counter. Travellers who skip this step use the touchscreen kiosks in the arrivals hall instead, which is fine but typically adds twenty to forty minutes at peak season. Our airport representative meets you after customs regardless of which route you take.\n\nA few important exceptions are worth knowing. Indian nationals do not need a visa at all and travel on a passport or voter ID. Nationals of SAARC countries and of China are granted a gratis tourist visa, and children under ten are free. Tourist visas can be extended in Kathmandu or Pokhara for USD 45 covering fifteen days plus USD 3 for each additional day, up to a total of 150 days in one calendar year. Finally, a short list of nationalities — including Afghanistan, Cameroon, Ghana, Iraq, Liberia, Nigeria, Palestine, Somalia, Syria, Ethiopia and Zimbabwe — cannot use visa on arrival and must obtain a visa in advance from a Nepali diplomatic mission. If you hold one of those passports, tell us at the enquiry stage and we will issue the trip documents you need for the application.',
