    id: 'info-002',
    title: 'Trekking Permits in Nepal, Explained',
    slug: 'trekking-permits-explained',
    category: 'permits',
    summary: 'TIMS cards, national park entry tickets, ACAP, municipality fees and restricted-area permits for Manaslu and Upper Mustang — what each one is, who issues it, roughly what it costs and which of them we arrange for you.',
    content: 'Nepal does not have one trekking permit. It has a small stack of them, and which ones you need depends entirely on where you walk. Every trekker we take into the hills carries at least two documents, and on restricted routes such as Manaslu and Upper Mustang the paperwork is genuinely complicated and can only be issued to a group travelling with a licensed agency. This page explains each permit honestly so you know what your trip price covers and what the checkpoint officer at Monjo, Dharapani or Kagbeni is actually looking at.\n\nThe Trekkers Information Management System card, universally called TIMS, is the baseline registration document issued jointly by the Nepal Tourism Board and the Trekking Agencies’ Association of Nepal. It exists for safety: your route, your emergency contact and your agency are recorded so that a search can begin quickly if you do not come out. It costs roughly NPR 2,000 for foreign nationals trekking through a registered agency and about NPR 1,000 for SAARC nationals, and it is checked at police posts along most trails. The Khumbu is the notable exception — the Khumbu Pasang Lhamu Rural Municipality collects its own local fee of about NPR 2,000 at Lukla or Monjo instead of TIMS.\n\nOn top of registration you pay a conservation or national park entry fee for the protected area you are entering. Sagarmatha National Park entry is around NPR 3,000 plus VAT and is issued at the Nepal Tourism Board office in Kathmandu or at the Monjo gate. The Annapurna Conservation Area Project permit, ACAP, is also around NPR 3,000 and covers the entire Annapurna Circuit, Annapurna Base Camp, Poon Hill and Mustang trails. Langtang National Park charges a comparable fee, and the Manaslu Conservation Area Project has its own permit on top of ACAP because the circuit begins in one protected area and finishes in another. These fees are collected once per entry, not per day, and children under ten are usually exempt.\n\nRestricted areas are a different category altogether. Upper Mustang, north of Kagbeni, requires a restricted-area permit costing USD 500 per person for ten days with USD 50 for each additional day, and it can only be issued to a minimum of two trekkers travelling with a government-licensed agency and a registered guide. Manaslu requires a restricted-area permit costing USD 100 per person for the first seven days in the September to November season, dropping to USD 75 in the quieter months, plus USD 15 or USD 10 per extra day. Upper Dolpo, Nar Phu, Tsum Valley and Kanchenjunga each have their own restricted-area rules. Solo trekking is not permitted in any of these zones, and no amount of persuasion at the checkpoint will change that.\n\nWe handle the entire permit process for every trip we operate. What we need from you is a clear colour scan of your passport photo page, your Nepal visa once it is stamped, two passport photographs, and your travel insurance certificate showing helicopter evacuation cover. We apply on your behalf in Kathmandu on the working day before departure and hand you the permits at the pre-trek briefing. Permits are personal, non-transferable and dated, so an itinerary change after issue sometimes means a fresh application — tell us early rather than at the trailhead. Government fees are reviewed periodically and the figures above are indicative; the current rate is confirmed in writing in your trip documents.',
