    id: 'info-004',
    title: 'Packing List for a Teahouse Trek',
    slug: 'teahouse-trek-packing-list',
    category: 'before-you-go',
    summary: 'A tested kit list for lodge-based trekking in the Everest, Annapurna, Langtang and Manaslu regions — the layering system that works, the weight limits that apply, what to rent in Kathmandu, and the things people always bring and never use.',
    content: 'Teahouse trekking in Nepal means you sleep in village lodges and eat cooked meals, so you are not carrying a tent, a stove or a fortnight of food. What you are carrying is a weather system: mornings at 3,900 metres that start at minus eight and afternoons at 2,600 metres that hit twenty-two degrees, sometimes on the same day. The kit that works is a layering system you can open and close while walking, not a single heavy coat. Get the layers right and everything else on this list is detail.\n\nWeight is the constraint that shapes the list. A porter carries around 20 to 25 kilograms in total, shared between two trekkers, so your duffel should not exceed 12 to 15 kilograms. Flights to Lukla are stricter still: 10 kilograms of checked baggage plus 5 kilograms of hand luggage per person, with excess charged by the kilo at the counter and occasionally refused outright when the aircraft is loaded to its limit for the altitude. Anything you do not need on the trail stays in a locked store at your Kathmandu hotel at no cost. We give you a duffel bag at the briefing and a dry bag liner, because it does rain in the Himalaya.\n\nFour layers cover every condition on a standard teahouse trek. Next to skin, two or three merino or synthetic base layer tops and one pair of thermal bottoms — never cotton, which holds sweat and chills you the moment you stop. Mid-layer, a light fleece and a synthetic or down sweater. Insulation, a proper down jacket rated for around minus ten to minus twenty degrees for the cold mornings and the evenings in unheated dining rooms above 4,000 metres. Shell, a waterproof and breathable jacket and over-trousers that pack small. For legs, two pairs of soft trekking trousers and one pair of shorts for the lower valleys. Add a warm hat, a sun hat, a buff, liner gloves and insulated gloves, and four to five pairs of proper trekking socks.\n\nFootwear decides whether you enjoy the trek. Bring mid or high-cut trekking boots with ankle support, broken in over at least 50 kilometres of walking at home, plus light shoes or sandals for the evenings. The Khumbu and Manaslu trails are rough stone and glacial moraine, not groomed path. Two adjustable trekking poles genuinely spare your knees on the long descents from Thorong La and Larke La. A three or four-season sleeping bag rated to around minus ten degrees comfort is essential — lodge blankets are thin and rooms are unheated — and a silk liner adds warmth and keeps the bag clean.\n\nThe small things that matter most are sun protection and water treatment. At 5,000 metres the ultraviolet is brutal even in cloud: category 4 glacier sunglasses, factor 50 sunscreen, high-factor lip balm with a lip screen, and a wide-brimmed hat. Carry two one-litre bottles or a bottle plus a bladder, and a treatment method — a SteriPEN, chlorine dioxide drops or a filter — so you can drink boiled or treated water instead of buying bottled water, which is banned above Monjo in the Khumbu and discouraged throughout the Annapurna Conservation Area. A headtorch with spare batteries, a power bank of at least 20,000 mAh because lodge charging is metered and slow above Namche, and a small personal first aid kit with blister plasters, ibuprofen, rehydration salts and any personal medication complete the essentials.\n\nWhat you can skip: jeans, a second pair of boots, a large camera tripod, more than one book, hotel-size toiletries, and the two-kilogram expedition parka that people buy for a trek that never goes above 5,400 metres. Kathmandu’s Thamel district rents down jackets and sleeping bags of decent quality by the day, which is the sensible option if you trek once and have nowhere to store the gear at home. Bring your own boots, your own base layers and your own socks — those are the three things that must fit you and only you.',
