    summary: 'How we run low-impact trips: no single-use plastic bottles, ethical porter loads under the IPPG guidelines, waste carried out of the high valleys, fair local employment, and simple etiquette for mani walls, monasteries and photographs.',
    content: 'The Himalaya absorbs a great deal from visitors and gives back generously, but the margin is thin. Above the treeline everything a trekker brings in has to be carried out or burned, water sources feed villages downstream, and a single season of careless behaviour undoes years of community work. Leave No Trace was written for wilderness in North America, yet its seven principles translate almost perfectly to a trail that passes through inhabited villages every two hours. We apply them on every trip, and we ask our travellers to hold us to them.\n\nDrinking water is where a trekker makes the biggest single difference. Bottled water sold above Monjo in the Khumbu is banned outright by the Khumbu Pasang Lhamu Rural Municipality, and single-use plastic is actively discouraged throughout the Annapurna Conservation Area. Every lodge sells boiled water by the litre and safe refill stations operate at several villages on the Everest and Annapurna trails. Carry two litres of bottle capacity and a SteriPEN, filter or chlorine dioxide drops, and you will avoid roughly forty plastic bottles on a two-week trek. Take your batteries, blister packs, wet wipes and any foil packaging back to Kathmandu with you — the Sagarmatha Pollution Control Committee manages waste in the Khumbu, but nothing that cannot be burned or composted has anywhere to go above Namche.\n\nOur staff are the second measure of a responsible operator. We follow the International Porter Protection Group guidelines: loads capped at around 25 kilograms, proper boots, jacket, gloves, sunglasses and sleeping gear issued to every porter, insurance for all field staff without exception, and the same access to medical care and evacuation that our clients receive. Porters sleep indoors, not in dining rooms after the guests have gone to bed. Guides are licensed, trained in altitude first aid and wilderness first response, and paid a daily wage above the TAAN minimum whether or not you tip. If a porter falls ill, he descends with an escort and is treated — the trip does not continue at his expense.\n\nMoney kept in the valley is money that keeps the trail alive. We use family-run lodges rather than the largest concrete blocks, buy produce locally where the season allows, and employ guides and porters from the regions they work in — Sherpa staff in the Khumbu, Gurung and Magar staff in the Annapurna, Tamang staff in Langtang. Buying a handmade item directly from the maker at Namche or Marpha returns far more to the village than the same item bought in Thamel. Please do not give sweets, pens or money to children on the trail; it encourages begging and undermines school attendance. If you want to contribute, ask your guide about the village school or health post and give through it.\n\nCultural etiquette on the trail is easy and appreciated. Walk clockwise around mani walls, chortens and prayer wheels, keeping them on your right. Ask before photographing people, especially monks, elderly villagers and inside monasteries, and accept a refusal gracefully. Dress modestly in villages and remove boots and hats before entering a gompa. Do not touch anyone on the head or point the soles of your feet toward a shrine or a person. Greet people with namaste and both palms together — it costs nothing and changes the tone of every encounter. Never buy items made from wildlife, and report any offer of them to your guide.\n\nFinally, the quiet principles. Stay on the established trail even when it is muddy, because a parallel path doubles the erosion scar. Give way to loaded yaks and mule trains on the mountain side of the path, never the drop side. Use lodge toilets and, where there is genuinely no facility, dig a hole at least 15 centimetres deep and 50 metres from any water source and pack out the paper. Keep noise down after dark in villages where families sleep behind the dining room wall. Leave the flowers, the mani stones and the prayer flags exactly where you found them.',
