    id: 'info-007',
    title: 'Getting to Nepal and Flying Domestically',
    slug: 'getting-to-nepal-and-domestic-flights',
    category: 'before-you-go',
    summary: 'International routes into Kathmandu, what to expect at Tribhuvan, and an honest account of domestic flying — the Lukla shuttle from Ramechhap in peak season, baggage limits, weather delays and when to consider a helicopter.',
    content: 'Almost every visitor arrives at Tribhuvan International Airport in Kathmandu, which sits at 1,338 metres in a bowl of hills that makes the approach memorable in clear weather. There is no direct service from Europe, North America or Australia, so you will connect through a Gulf or Asian hub: Doha, Dubai, Abu Dhabi, Istanbul, Delhi, Bangkok, Kuala Lumpur, Singapore, Hong Kong, Guangzhou or Chengdu are the usual options, with Seoul and Tokyo connecting via Bangkok, Hong Kong or Delhi. Qatar Airways, Turkish Airlines, Himalaya Airlines, Nepal Airlines, Thai, Malaysia Airlines, AirAsia, IndiGo, Air India and Cathay all serve Kathmandu. Two newer international airports — Pokhara Regional and Gautam Buddha at Bhairahawa near Lumbini — handle limited regional traffic, so Kathmandu remains the practical gateway.\n\nTribhuvan is compact and can be slow when several wide-bodies land together. Budget ninety minutes from wheels-down to the arrivals hall in high season: visa form, fee counter, immigration, baggage and a customs scan. Our airport representative waits in the car park just beyond the terminal doors with a Camp for Nepal board — vehicles are not permitted at the kerb, so walk out through the exit gate and look left. Transfers to Thamel take between twenty-five minutes and an hour depending on traffic. A local SIM card from Ncell or Nepal Telecom is sold at the airport and in Thamel for a few dollars and works surprisingly well along the main trekking valleys, though coverage disappears in side valleys and above 4,500 metres.\n\nDomestic flying is where Nepal asks for patience. Buddha Air, Yeti Airlines, Shree Airlines, Tara Air, Summit Air and Nepal Airlines fly Kathmandu to Pokhara, Bhairahawa, Bharatpur, Nepalgunj, Biratnagar, Simikot and the mountain strips. Standard domestic baggage is 20 kilograms checked plus 5 kilograms hand luggage, but mountain sectors are stricter. Flights to Lukla and Jomsom carry 10 kilograms checked and 5 kilograms hand luggage per person, and the limit is real — these aircraft operate close to their performance ceiling and excess is either charged by the kilo or offloaded. Everything you do not need on the trail stays in the hotel store room in Kathmandu.\n\nLukla deserves its own explanation. Tenzing-Hillary Airport sits at 2,860 metres with a 527-metre sloping runway and no instrument approach, so flights operate on visual rules in the morning window before cloud and valley wind build. During the busiest weeks of the autumn and spring seasons, when Kathmandu air traffic is saturated, Lukla flights are moved to Manthali airport in Ramechhap, a four to five-hour drive east of Kathmandu that we start at around one in the morning to make the first departure slot. It is a genuinely tiring transfer and we will tell you honestly if your dates fall inside that window. Delays of a day or more happen every season in both directions, which is the single strongest argument for building buffer days into your itinerary and for holding a flexible international ticket.\n\nHelicopters are the practical alternative when fixed-wing flights are grounded for consecutive days, and a shared charter out of Lukla is a routine option at the end of a trek. They are not merely a luxury: a helicopter can often fly in marginal conditions that ground the aeroplanes, and a shared seat is usually available at short notice. Our team in Kathmandu monitors Lukla operations every morning of your trek and will propose alternatives before you are stuck. For Pokhara, the road option is a genuinely pleasant 200-kilometre drive of six to seven hours on the Prithvi Highway, or a tourist coach, or a 25-minute flight with Himalayan views on the right-hand side going out and the left coming back.',
